def euler_char(word):
    n = len(word)
    parent = list(range(n))

    def find(x):
        while parent[x] != x:
            x = parent[x]
        return x

    ends = {}
    for i, ch in enumerate(word):
        tail, head = i, (i + 1) % n
        if ch.isupper():
            tail, head = head, tail
        ends.setdefault(ch.lower(), []).append((tail, head))
    for (t1, h1), (t2, h2) in ends.values():
        parent[find(t1)] = find(t2)
        parent[find(h1)] = find(h2)
    v = len({find(x) for x in range(n)})
    return v - n // 2 + 1
```

For `'abab'` the corners fall into two classes, so $\chi = 2 - 2 + 1 = 1$: the projective plane. Corner-chasing by hand is exactly this union–find computation.
